import json
N=[]
def n(id,para,lemma,side,typ,text,status="certain"):
    N.append({"id":id,"anchor":{"para":para,"lemma":lemma,"side":side},"type":typ,"status":status,"text":text})
# s001
n("n001","s001-h","Alfonso de Aragonia","la","historical","Alfonso (Alonso) de Aragón (1470–1520), natural son of Ferdinand II of Aragon and Aldonza Ruiz de Ivorra. Provided to the see of Zaragoza by Sixtus IV on 14 August 1478, aged about eight, initially as administrator; ordained priest and bishop only on 7–8 November 1501; archbishop of Valencia 1512; d. 24 February 1520. In May 1486 he was about sixteen, which explains the comparison with royal-born *adolescentes* at §s001-05. Sources: Catholic-Hierarchy (catholic-hierarchy.org/bishop/bdearagon.html); Fernández Serrano, *Cuadernos de Historia Jerónimo Zurita* 19–20.","verified")
n("n002","s001-h","cesaraugustanensi","la","textual","Printed with a suspension mark (*cesaraugustanē*); expanded *cesaraugustanensi*, ‘of Caesaraugusta’, i.e. Zaragoza.")
n("n003","s001-h","commendat","la","textual","Copy-specific: after *commendat* a later hand has added in ink ‘F. X. S.’ followed by a row of x-like marks; to the right, an oval ownership stamp with a crowned monogram. At the foot, a manuscript note ‘Tolosa 1486’ and a circular stamp whose legend is only partly legible (‘…GAS DAL…’). None of this belongs to the printed text; to be described in the introduction (provenance).")
n("n004","s001-h","Guillermus Gorris","la","historical","On the author’s order there is disagreement: Catalan scholarship on Scotism (in the journal *Enrahonar*) calls him ‘fra Guillem Gorris, de la Província d’Aragó’, whereas the *Catholic Encyclopedia* article ‘Scotism and Scotists’ states that he was *not* a Franciscan. The incipit (§s002-t) styles him only ‘professor of arts and sacred theology’ and ‘Aragonese’. To be resolved from primary evidence.","to-verify")
n("n005","s001-01","natus","la","textual","Print damaged at line-end (‘nat’ + stain before the colon). *natus* is the expected reading but cannot be fully seen.","uncertain")
n("n006","s001-01","generali vicario","la","historical","The vicar general of the archdiocese of Zaragoza. As Alfonso held the see as a minor administrator, government was exercised through vicars general; the individual meant here is not yet identified.","conjecture")
n("n007","s001-01","Sed quia","la","textual","Printed *q* with the r-rotunda-shaped sign (qꝛ), the standard abbreviation for *quia* (cf. Cappelli).")
n("n008","s001-01","propria carens patria","la","historical","Gorris writes from Toulouse (cf. §s001-06 ‘Tholosanis vigiliis’), i.e. away from his native Aragon.")
n("n009","s001-02","summulatim","la","textual","Print reads *sumulatim* (possibly with a faint nasal stroke). Taken as *summulatim*, ‘in the form of summaries’, a rare adverb from *summula*; *simulatim* is less likely in context. Translation follows *summulatim*.","uncertain")
n("n010","s001-02","puteo","la","translation","The image of drawing water from a deep, dark well into a shallow one is Gorris’s programme for the whole book: Scotus’s doctrine made accessible by abridgement. Cf. the ‘rivulus … a fonte’ image at §s002-09.")
n("n011","s001-02","prelate","en","translation","Lat. *pontifex*, in fifteenth-century usage simply ‘bishop’; rendered ‘prelate’ to avoid the papal connotation of ‘pontiff’.")
n("n012","s001-02","in such a spirit","en","translation","Lat. *quam vellem sic legas, vt eam tenuem et inopem nec scriptione magnopere dignam iudices*: a modesty topos; the construction is loose and the translation interprets it.")
n("n013","s001-03","mortalium","la","textual","Printed *mortaliū*. Grammar expects *omnes mortales* (subject of *indicant*); possibly a compositor’s error, or an ellipse (*omnes mortalium [linguae]*). Kept as printed; translated as if *mortales*.","uncertain")
n("n014","s001-03","insectatio","la","textual","So printed. *insectatio* normally means ‘pursuit, hostile pursuit’; here apparently ‘zealous pursuit (of studies)’. A misprint for *inspectio* / *inspectatio* (‘scrutiny’) cannot be excluded.","uncertain")
n("n015","s001-03","castigauerit","la","textual","Printed *castigauēt* with r-abbreviation; expanded *castigauerit*.")
n("n016","s001-04","num ius ciuile","la","textual","Question mark supplied by the editor; the print has a colon after *potest*.")
n("n017","s001-04","vnumque","la","textual","Printed *vnūq3*. Read *vnumque* (‘and one [single] light’); *vnumquemque* is palaeographically possible but gives poorer sense.","uncertain")
n("n018","s001-05","antistatum","la","textual","So printed. Apparently a noun formed from *antistare* (‘to excel’), ‘pre-eminence’, parallel to *principatum*. Not in classical lexica; kept.","uncertain")
n("n019","s001-05","transhominem","la","translation","A striking coinage, *trans hominem*, ‘beyond the human’; rendered ‘more than human’.")
n("n020","s001-06","Tholosanis vigiliis","la","historical","The book was printed at Toulouse by Henricus (Heinrich) Mayer; the Biblioteca Nacional de Portugal record of this copy (INC. 535, purl.pt/32008) dates it ‘posterior a 10 Maio 1486’, i.e. after the date of this letter; [256] leaves, 4°. A later edition (Speyer: Peter Drach, c. 1492; GW 10961) and a manuscript copy of c. 1490 (Edinburgh, National Library of Scotland, ‘Scotus Pauperum’, from the Carmelites of Baccarat) are potential collation witnesses.","verified")
n("n021","s001-06","decimo May","la","historical","10 May 1486: the letter’s date and the terminus post quem of the printing. *May* = *Maii*, printed thus.")
n("n022","s001-06","sensu habundas","la","translation","*sensus* here in the sense of judgement/understanding (cf. *sensus communis*), not sense-perception.")
# s002
n("n023","s002-t","Scotus pauperum","la","translation","‘Scotus of / for the Poor’: the *pauperes* are students who cannot afford, or have no time for, the many volumes required (cf. §s002-02 *ob plurimorum librorum reuolutionem*). The title places the work in the tradition of compendia ‘for the poor’ such as the *Biblia pauperum* and the *Thesaurus pauperum*.")
n("n024","s002-t","quatuor libris sententiarum","la","source","Peter Lombard, *Sententiae in IV libris distinctae*. Which Scotist text Gorris abridges (Ordinatio / *Opus Oxoniense*, or the *Reportata Parisiensia*, in a fifteenth-century printed form) is to be established; the opening question (§s003-01) corresponds to *Ordinatio*, Prologus, pars 1, q. unica.","to-verify")
n("n025","s002-01","Quanquam","la","textual","A space was left by the printer for the initial, supplied in this copy by hand as a red *Q* (likewise the red *C* at §s003-01).")
n("n026","s002-01","coagulandas","la","translation","Lit. ‘to be curdled, congealed’, i.e. condensed; rendered ‘to condense’.")
n("n027","s002-01","cum earum non parua semita","la","textual","Elliptical (no verb); sense apparently ‘since the path [through them] is no short one’. Translation conjectural.","uncertain")
n("n028","s002-01","peregrinantium","la","translation","‘Those who travel’: plausibly wandering scholars or mendicants who cannot carry a library; Gorris returns to them at §s002-02.","conjecture")
n("n029","s002-02","Eas ob res","la","textual","Divided in the print across a line-break (*ob | res*); not *obres*.")
n("n030","s002-02","Citacorum","la","textual","Printed *Citacoꝝ*. Unidentified. Possibly for *psittacorum*/*sitacorum* (‘of parrots’), hence the hesitant translation; *cicadarum* is palaeographically less likely. The bee-image of gathering flowers into one place is a classical commonplace for compilation (Seneca, *Ep.* 84; Macrobius, *Saturnalia*, praef.).","uncertain")
n("n031","s002-02","preeligendos","la","textual","*p* with a superscript stroke: read *pre-* (*preeligendos*, ‘to be picked out’); *per-* (*perlegendos*) is not excluded.","uncertain")
n("n032","s002-02","alueum","la","translation","*alveus*: hollow, channel, or beehive; rendered ‘hive’ to keep the bee simile.")
n("n033","s002-02","subtilissimi sacre theologie doctoris","la","historical","John Duns Scotus (c. 1265/66–1308), the *Doctor Subtilis*.")
n("n034","s002-03","Si alterum recta intelligentia","la","translation","*alterum … alterum*: taken as referring respectively to the conclusions of arguments and the abridgements of opinions.","conjecture")
n("n035","s002-04","ortor","la","textual","First word of p. 4, printed thus: = *hortor*, without the aspirate.")
n("n036","s002-05","coram tondente","la","source","Isaiah 53:7 (Vulg.): *quasi agnus coram tondente se obmutescet et non aperiet os suum*; cf. Acts 8:32.","verified")
n("n037","s002-06","fauoris","la","textual","The type appears to read *fouoris*: either a misprint or a damaged *a*. Read *fauoris*.","uncertain")
n("n038","s002-06","licet minimus tamen indignus","la","textual","The sense (‘though least, yet unworthy’) is awkward; perhaps for *et* or *tanquam*. Translated freely (‘and unworthy withal’).","uncertain")
n("n039","s002-06","sacre pagine magistros","la","translation","*magister sacrae paginae*: the standard title of a master (doctor) of theology.")
n("n040","s002-06","corpora","la","translation","Literally ‘bodies’; whether Gorris means the hearers’ bodies (prosperity, honour) or ‘bodies’ of learning is unclear. Translated literally.","uncertain")
n("n041","s002-08","mater est et magistra","la","source","The formula *omnium fidelium mater et magistra* for the Roman Church is standard (cf. Fourth Lateran Council, const. 5).","to-verify")
n("n042","s002-09","prout maximo legum latori","la","source","Gorris is quoting the Digest. Cf. Gaius, *Dig.* 1.2.1: *quanto magis interpretationem promittentibus inconveniens erit omissis initiis atque origine non repetita … protinus materiam interpretationis tractare? namque nisi fallor istae praefationes et libentius nos ad lectionem propositae materiae producunt et cum ibi venerimus, evidentiorem praestant intellectum*; and Pomponius, *Dig.* 1.2.2 pr.: *Necessarium itaque nobis videtur ipsius iuris originem atque processum demonstrare. Et quidem …*. The ‘greatest lawgiver’ is presumably Justinian, in whose name the Digest speaks (or Gaius himself).","verified")
n("n043","s002-09","epigrama","la","translation","*epigramma* in the sense of ‘title, inscription’.")
n("n044","s002-09","scotizabunt","la","translation","*scotizare*, ‘to scotize’: to follow, or think along with, Scotus. Kept as a neologism in quotation marks; cf. *scotizarent* §s002-10.")
n("n045","s002-10","doctoris subtilis","la","translation","Scotus’s customary epithet; capitalised in the translation (‘the Subtle Doctor’).")
n("n046","s002-10","articulos Scoti","la","translation","‘Articles’: the subdivisions of Scotus’s questions in the edition(s) Gorris used.")
n("n047","s002-11","Summa autem diuisio","la","source","A fourfold summary of the *Sentences* (Trinity; creation; Incarnation; sacraments and resurrection) attributed to Scotus (*prout a Scoto traditur*). The exact source has not yet been identified.","to-verify")
n("n048","s002-11","ab vtroque","la","source","The *Filioque*: procession of the Holy Spirit from Father and Son (Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 11).")
# s003
n("n049","s003-h","Prologus","la","historical","Beginning of the commentary proper (p. 6 = a iii verso). Headed *Prologus*; hand-supplied red initial *C* at the start of the question.")
n("n050","s003-01","vtrum homini pro statu isto","la","source","= Scotus, *Ordinatio*, Prologus, pars 1, q. unica, n. 1 (Vatican ed., vol. I): *Quaeritur utrum homini pro statu isto sit necessarium aliquam doctrinam specialem supernaturaliter inspirari, ad quam videlicet non posset attingere lumine naturali intellectus.* Gorris reads *supernaturalem* for *supernaturaliter* and *possit* for *posset*.","verified")
n("n051","s003-01","in the present state","en","translation","*pro statu isto*: Scotus’s technical expression for the condition of the wayfarer in this life (as opposed to the state of innocence or of glory). Rendered ‘in the present state’, as is standard in Scotist English.")
n("n052","s003-02","Videtur quod sic","la","textual","The *Ordinatio* reads *Et quod non, arguo sic*. The argument concludes that no supernatural doctrine is necessary, so *sic* must mean ‘that it is so [sc. not necessary]’ or is a slip for *non*. Kept as printed; to be checked against the 1492 edition and the NLS manuscript.","uncertain")
n("n053","s003-02","being qua being","en","translation","*ens inquantum ens*, the first (adequate) natural object of the intellect for Scotus; rendered ‘being *qua* being’.")
n("n054","s003-02","potest in illud","la","translation","Scholastic *posse in* + acc.: ‘to have power over, extend to’ an object; rendered ‘can extend to’.")
n("n055","s003-02","2° de anima","la","source","Aristotle, *De anima* II. Scotus (*Ord.* prol. n. 1) proves the major by the example of the first object of sight, which points to *De anima* II.7 (418a26 ff.). Exact locus intended by Gorris to be confirmed.","conjecture")
n("n056","s003-02","Auicenne primo methaphisice c. 5°","la","source","Avicenna, *Liber de philosophia prima* I, c. 5: *ens et res … prima impressione imprimuntur in anima*; cited by Scotus at the same point (*Ord.* prol. n. 1). Abbreviated in the print as *metha.*; expanded *methaphisice*.","verified")
n("n057","s003-03","non est qui possit scire vias eius","la","source","Baruch 3:31 (Vulg.); cited by Scotus *ad oppositum*, *Ord.* prol. n. 4. *eius* refers to wisdom, hence ‘her ways’.","verified")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Revisions after collation with B (Speyer: Peter Drach, c. 1492; BSB 4 Inc.s.a. 910 a)
def upd(id, text=None, status=None, append=None):
    for x in N:
        if x["id"]==id:
            if text is not None: x["text"]=text
            if append: x["text"]+=" "+append
            if status: x["status"]=status
            return
    raise KeyError(id)
upd("n005", append="B reads *natus* unambiguously, confirming the reading.", status="verified")
upd("n009", append="B prints *sūmulatim* (= *summulatim*), confirming the reading.", status="verified")
upd("n013", append="B has the same *mortaliū iudicant*, so the anomaly is not a Toulouse misprint; B’s *iudicant* (‘judge’) is the better verb (A *indicant*, ‘declare’; both make sense).")
upd("n018", append="B reads *antistitum* (‘of prelates’): ‘[among] prelates you hold [such pre-eminence] in virtues’. B’s reading is easier and may be right; A is kept as copy-text, but the translation is compatible with either.")
upd("n020", text="The book was printed at Toulouse by Henricus (Heinrich) Mayer; the Biblioteca Nacional de Portugal record of this copy (INC. 535, purl.pt/32008) dates it ‘posterior a 10 Maio 1486’, i.e. after the date of this letter; [256] leaves, 4°. Collated here with B = the Speyer reprint ([Peter Drach, c. 1492]; GW 10961; Munich BSB 4 Inc.s.a. 910 a), which omits the dating clause *Datum Tholose … lxxxvi°* altogether. A manuscript copy of c. 1490 (Edinburgh, NLS, from the Carmelites of Baccarat) has not yet been consulted.", status="verified")
upd("n024", text="Peter Lombard, *Sententiae in IV libris distinctae*. The text Gorris abridges is Scotus’s *Ordinatio* (*Opus Oxoniense*): his lemmata in the Prologue reproduce its wording verbatim, e.g. *Dicta tamen responsio ad secundam rationem* (§s003-08 = *Ord.* prol. n. 80), *Alia datur responsio quod ex solis maioribus* (§s003-09 = n. 85), *Ad primum distinguo de obiecto naturali* (§s003-10 = n. 90). Which printed or manuscript form of the *Ordinatio* he used remains to be established (note that his argument-numbering is one lower than the Vatican edition’s).", status="verified")
upd("n030", append="B prints the same unintelligible form, so the crux goes back to the copy-text and is not resolved by collation.")
upd("n031", append="B: *preeligendos* in full, confirming the reading.", status="verified")
upd("n037", append="B: *fauoris*, confirming the reading.", status="verified")
upd("n038", append="B reads the same phrase (misprinted *mimus* for *minimus*), so the wording is authorial; the awkwardness is Gorris’s own.", status="certain")
upd("n049", text="Beginning of the commentary proper (p. 6, verso). Headed *Prologus*; hand-supplied red initial *C* at the start of the question. B adds no separate heading here.")
upd("n052", text="So A. B reads *Videtur quod non*, and the *Ordinatio* (prol. n. 1) *Et quod non, arguo sic*: the argument concludes that no supernatural doctrine is necessary. A’s *sic* is kept as copy-text; the translation follows B and the *Ordinatio* (see the English note on ‘It seems not’).", status="verified")

# --- s001 additional B variants
n("n058","s001-02","in alium non profundum puteolum luculenter transferendam","la","textual","B omits *et in alium … transferendam* (the second half of the well image), leaving only *ab obscuro et centro terre proximo puteo exhauriendam decreui*.")
n("n059","s001-02","reuerendissimo et illustrissimo pontifici","la","textual","B: *tibi reuerendissimo pontifici* (om. *et illustrissimo*).")
n("n060","s001-02","theologica Scoti doctrina","la","textual","B: *theologia scoti. doctrina* (with a stop), apparently a misreading of A’s adjective.")
n("n061","s001-02","pro fugata","la","textual","B prints *profugata* as one word. Read either way the sense is ‘that, negligence being put to flight, I should no longer hold back’.")
n("n062","s001-03","atque regalem","la","textual","B omits *atque regalem*: *tuam archiepiscopalem dignitatem*.")
n("n063","s001-03","iniurias","la","textual","B: *iniuriam*.")
n("n064","s001-04","cognosci potest","la","textual","B: *sciri potest*.")
n("n065","s001-h","subditus","la","textual","On *Guillermus*: B prints *Guillerinus* (*Guillerin⁹*), a compositor’s misreading of *Guillermus*; also *alfonsio de aragonia archiepiscopo de cesaraugustanensi*.")
n("n066","s001-01","admonitum fuisse","la","textual","B: *admonitum me fuisse*, supplying the subject of the infinitive.")

# --- s003 (Prologue q.1 = Ord. prol. p.1 q.un.)
n("n067","s003-02","It seems not","en","translation","The translation follows B (*Videtur quod non*) and the *Ordinatio* (*Et quod non*) against A’s *Videtur quod sic*; see Latin note on *Videtur quod sic*.")
n("n068","s003-02","Sed potentia nostra","la","textual","B: *Sed potentia nostra intellectiua*, with the *Ordinatio* (n. 1: *sed intellectus noster*).")
n("n069","s003-04","Prima opinio","la","source","‘Two pages further on’: *Ord.* prol. n. 42, *Contra istam quaestionem arguo, et probo quod quaecumque necessaria …*. Gorris’s ‘first opinion’ is Henry of Ghent’s position as reported and attacked by Scotus at nn. 40–48; cf. Henry, *Summa quaestionum ordinariarum* a. 1 q. 4 (attribution as given by the Vatican editors; not yet checked in Henry).","verified")
n("n070","s003-04","Henricus serbonicus","la","historical","Henry of Ghent (d. 1293), *doctor solemnis*. ‘Sorbonist’ is a late-medieval tag (he was associated with the Sorbonne milieu and a benefactor of it is sometimes claimed); Gorris’s etymology ‘from his college … from his homeland’ is his own.","to-verify")
n("n071","s003-04","incipit. hec ratio est contra philosophos","la","textual","B: *quedam incipit*, ‘a certain [argument] begins’, and prints *in scoto* for A’s *in Scoto*. Gorris refers to the opening of Scotus’s rebuttal, *Ord.* prol. n. 43 ff.")
n("n072","s003-05","quarto sic arguitur","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 49 (*Quarto sic: omne agens per cognitionem …*, Vatican wording differs slightly). Gorris assigns it to Thomas, *Summa theologiae* I q. 1 a. 1 corp.: *homo ordinatur ad Deum sicut ad quendam finem qui comprehensionem rationis excedit*; the Vatican apparatus gives the same reference.","certain")
n("n073","s003-06","quinto arguo sic. nullum agens","la","textual","B: *Quinto arguitur sic. Omne agens*, agreeing with the *Ordinatio* (n. 51); A’s *nullum* is a slip.")
n("n074","s003-06","in §. hec ratio videtur concludere contra eum","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 52: *Haec ratio videtur concludere contra eum …*. The ‘him’ attacked is the author of the argument (Henry/Ware), whose own principles would forbid such knowledge.","verified")
n("n075","s003-06","Guillermi varronis in li. 1. q. 2","la","source","William of Ware OFM (fl. c. 1290–1305), Scotus’s supposed master, *In I Sent.*, prol. q. 2 (unedited; numbered differently in the manuscripts). Gorris names Ware constantly as Scotus’s silent interlocutor, an important testimony to how fifteenth-century Scotists read the *Ordinatio*.","to-verify")
n("n076","s003-08","quere responsum Thome","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 79: *Ad rationem tertiam quaere responsionem Thomae in Summa I q. 1*. Gorris calls it ‘the second’ because he counts the arguments one lower than the Vatican edition.","verified")
n("n077","s003-08","q. liiii. arti. 2°","la","source","Thomas, *ST* I q. 54 a. 2 (‘whether the action of an angel is its being’) as the source of the ‘second reply’ (*Ord.* prol. n. 80, *Dicta tamen responsio*). The pertinence of this article is not obvious and the reference may be corrupt.","to-verify")
n("n078","s003-08","Godofredi quodlibeto. 14. q. 4. seu. c. 9","la","source","Godfrey of Fontaines, *Quodlibet* XIV? Godfrey’s quodlibets number fifteen; the locus is not yet identified. B prints the reference without *seu c. 9*.","to-verify")
n("n079","s003-09","ex ⟨propositiuo⟩ prima parte summe","la","textual","A prints *ppositiuo* (*p* with stroke). The form is meaningless as it stands. B reads *ex ppositio in .i. parte sum.*, i.e. *ex proposito in prima parte summe*, ‘expressly in the first part of the *Summa*’, which the translation adopts. Alternatively *Praepositiuo*, i.e. Praepositinus of Cremona’s *Summa*; but the ‘first part of the *Summa*’ strongly suggests Thomas again (*ST* I q. 1). Cf. *Ord.* prol. n. 84.","conjecture")
n("n080","s003-09","Alia datur responsio","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 85, verbatim (*Alia datur responsio quod ex solis maioribus …*). B: *ex maioribus solum*.","verified")
n("n081","s003-10","Ad primum distinguo de obiecto naturali","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 90, Scotus’s reply to the first principal argument. B omits *ex fratre* in the following sentence.","verified")
n("n082","s003-11","Que inquit causa","la","source","Augustine, *De Trinitate* XV.27.50 (the last chapter, as Gorris says): *Quae igitur causa est cur acie fixa ipsam videre non possis nisi utique infirmitas, et quis eam tibi fecit nisi utique iniquitas?* Gorris quotes freely (*possumus*, *non nisi*). Scotus does not cite this text here; it is Gorris’s own addition. Checked against the Latin Library text of *De Trin.* XV.","verified")
n("n083","s003-12","potentia receptiua siue intellectiua","la","source","Summary of *Ord.* prol. nn. 57–62, Scotus’s distinction of natural and supernatural by comparison of the receptive power to the act received and to the agent; ‘God begets’, ‘God is three and one’ are Scotus’s examples.","verified")
n("n084","s003-13","sub ratione entis","la","source","Cf. *Ord.* prol. n. 90 ff. (reply to the first argument): the intellect can know God under the general concept of being, but not under the proper concept of deity.","verified")
n("n085","s003-11","answers the title","en","translation","*respondet affirmatiue titulo*: ‘answers the [question’s] title affirmatively’, i.e. Scotus holds that supernatural doctrine is necessary.")
n("n086","s003-09","⟨expressly⟩","en","translation","Translating the conjecture *ex proposito* (from B’s *ex ppositio*); see the Latin note on *propositiuo*.")
n("n087","s003-12","agent intellect and the phantasm","en","translation","*intellectus agens et fantasma*, the two natural concauses of intellection in Scotus; whatever exceeds their causality is ‘supernatural’ to the intellect.")
n("n088","s003-06","speaks against him","en","translation","A difficult sentence: *Dum contra eum dicit doctor subtilis* seems to identify the person against whom Scotus turns the argument (cf. *Ord.* prol. n. 52). The bracket makes the reference explicit.")

# --- s004 (Prologue q.2 = Ord. prol. p.2 q.un.)
n("n089","s004-01","vtrum cognitio supernaturalis","la","source","= *Ord.* prol. p. 2 q. un., n. 95: *Utrum cognitio supernaturalis necessaria viatori sit sufficienter tradita in sacra Scriptura*.","verified")
n("n090","s004-02","His fidem habemus","la","source","Augustine, *De civitate Dei* XI.3: *huic [scripturae] fidem habemus de his rebus quas ignorare non expedit nec per nos nosse idonei sumus*; cited at *Ord.* prol. n. 98. Gorris reads *His*; B *Hic*. The verb *nosse* has dropped out in A.","verified")
n("n091","s004-03","libro Augustini de heresibus","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 99 names Augustine *and* Damascene *De haeresibus* (B: *aug. et damas.*); Gorris’s A drops Damascene. The examples (Manichaeans via *De utilitate credendi*; Jas 5:16) are Scotus’s.","verified")
n("n092","s004-03","dispensari a quocunque non sacerdote","la","source","Jas 5:16; the heresy is that confession may be made to, and absolution given by, a layman (*Ord.* prol. n. 99: *dicendo illud posse a quocumque non sacerdote …*).","verified")
n("n093","s004-04","10. de ciui. dei in fi.","la","source","Scotus (*Ord.* prol. n. 101) cites Augustine, *De civ. Dei* XII.10: *vera esse praeterita ex his quae futura praenuntiavit, cum tanta veritate implentur, ostendit*; B agrees (*xii … c. x*). A’s ‘X, at the end’ garbles this reference; the translation keeps A’s wording.","verified")
n("n094","s004-04","18. de ciui. dei. c. 43","la","source","Augustine, *De civ. Dei* XVIII.41–43 (concord of the canonical writers, the Septuagint); *Ord.* prol. n. 102 cites XVIII.42 (B: *c. xlij*).","certain")
n("n095","s004-04","li. 1. c. 3°","la","source","Richard of St Victor, *De Trinitate*, prologue/ I c. 2 is cited by Scotus at nn. 105 and 113; ‘c. 3’ in A may be a slip. The quotation ‘a summe sanctitatis viris’ paraphrases Richard’s prologue.","to-verify")
n("n096","s004-05","diuina miseratione","la","textual","So A; B and the *Ordinatio* (n. 107, after Augustine, *De civ.* XVIII.38): *diuina inspiratione*. The translation follows *inspiratione*.")
n("n097","s004-05","Dieronimum","la","textual","A’s *Dieronimum* is a misprint (D for H); B *Hieronimum*. Augustine, *Ep.* 82.1.3 to Jerome; in Gratian, *Decretum* D. 9 c. 5 (*Ego solis*). Cited at *Ord.* prol. n. 107.")
n("n098","s004-05","dedisti hunc honorem diferre","la","textual","Augustine, *Ep.* 82.3: *didici hunc timorem honoremque deferre*. A’s *dedisti … diferre* is corrupt; B *Didici … deferre*. Translation follows Augustine and B. Checked against augustinus.it.","verified")
n("n099","s004-05","quantalibus sanctitate doctrinarum","la","textual","Augustine: *quantalibet sanctitate doctrinaque praepolleant*. A corrupt; B *quanta sanctitate quantaue doctrina*. Translation follows Augustine.","verified")
n("n100","s004-05","scriptura Enoch","la","source","Augustine, *De civ. Dei* XVIII.38 on the Book of Enoch (Jude 14) and the lost books cited in Kings; *Ord.* prol. n. 107.","certain")
n("n101","s004-06","Mathei. 7. Hoc fac et viues","la","textual","The quotation *Hoc fac et viues* is Luke 10:28, not Matthew 7; B reads *Lu. x*. Scotus (*Ord.* prol. n. 108) cites Matt 7:12 (*Hoc facias alii …*, the Golden Rule). A conflates the two.","verified")
n("n102","s004-06","quo nihil est vanius","la","textual","B: *nihil est vanius* without *quo*. The phrase replaces Scotus’s *in quibus nihil est numinis* (*Ord.* prol. n. 109).")
n("n103","s004-06","Sardanapolo","la","source","Aristotle, *Nicomachean Ethics* I.5, 1095b19–22 (Sardanapallus as the type of the life of pleasure). Scotus’s text at n. 109 speaks rather of the Saracens’ ‘gluttony and coition’ and cites Avicenna, *Metaph.* IX; Gorris transfers the charge to the pagans.","certain")
n("n104","s004-06","spectant","la","textual","B: *expectant*, ‘await’, which the translation follows.")
n("n105","s004-07","Aug. 8° de vtilitate credendi","la","source","Augustine, *De utilitate credendi* 17.35 (*Dubitabimus nos eius Ecclesiae condere gremio …*), cited at *Ord.* prol. n. 111. ‘8°’ is not a book number (the work has one book); perhaps a chapter number in Gorris’s source.","to-verify")
n("n106","s004-07","si est error a te decepti sumus","la","source","Richard of St Victor, *De Trinitate* I.2: *Domine, si error est, a te decepti sumus …*; *Ord.* prol. n. 113.","certain")
n("n107","s004-08","Christus hic erat","la","source","Josephus, *Antiquitates* XVIII.3.3 (the *Testimonium Flavianum*); *Ord.* prol. n. 118. B: *c. xxxiii*.","certain")
n("n108","s004-08","Sibille de ciuitate. 18. c. 23","la","source","Augustine, *De civ. Dei* XVIII.23 (the Erythraean Sibyl’s acrostic ΙΧΘΥΣ); *Ord.* prol. n. 118.","certain")
n("n109","s004-08","querentibus corde","la","textual","B and the *Ordinatio* (n. 119): *querentibus toto corde*.")
n("n110","s004-09","sexcentis annis post legem Christi","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 112 (under Scotus’s seventh way, as Gorris says): *illa incepit plusquam sexcentis annis post legem Christi, et in brevi, Domino volente, finietur, quia multum debilitata est anno Christi millesimo trecentesimo*.","verified")
n("n111","s004-09","⟨Abynotus⟩","la","textual","A *Abynot⁹*, B *abynorus*. Evidently Hayton (Hethum) of Corycus, the Armenian prince-historian, author of the *Flos historiarum terre Orientis* (1307). He was a kinsman (nephew) of King Hethum I, not ‘brother of the king’, and the work was presented to Pope Clement V, not to Philip IV of France, although it circulated with French royal patronage. The form of the name is unexplained.","conjecture")
n("n112","s004-10","apud nos","la","textual","Scotus (*Ord.* prol. n. 112): *prophetia dicitur apud eos esse*, ‘among them [the Saracens]’. Gorris’s lemma reads *apud nos*.")
n("n113","s004-10","viagio sancti Jacobi","la","source","The *Liber Sancti Jacobi* (*Codex Calixtinus*), ascribed to Pope Calixtus II; Book IV, the Pseudo-Turpin, c. 4 *De ydolo Mahummet*: the idol *Salam Cadis* at Cádiz holding a great key that will fall in the year a future king of the Franks is born. Checked against the Pseudo-Turpin text (ed. Smyser; skaldic.org text).","verified")
n("n114","s004-10","Salez cadis","la","textual","A *Salez cadis*, B *saulem cadis*; Pseudo-Turpin *Salam Cadis*.","uncertain")
n("n115","s004-10","⟨ruet⟩","la","textual","A prints *met* (inverted *u*?); B *ruet*, adopted. The clause is elliptic: in the Pseudo-Turpin the key falls *anno quo rex futurus in Gallia natus fuerit*; Gorris leaves the time-clause unfinished.")
n("n116","s004-10","sed deus cadis est mons","la","textual","Pseudo-Turpin: *Cadis dicitur locus … Salam in lingua arabica Deus dicitur*. Gorris’s sentence seems to have misplaced *deus*, which belongs with *salem* (‘*salem* in Arabic means God’); *mons* for ‘place’ is his own. *deus* is therefore probably intrusive here.","conjecture")
n("n117","s004-11","millesimum quingentesimum","la","textual","A: 1500; B: *millesimum sexcentesimum* (1600). B’s change looks like an updating of a failed prophecy-date, but 1600 could also be the original. The source of the astrological prophecy is not identified.","uncertain")
n("n118","s004-12","Reliquie israel conuertentur in finem","la","source","Paraphrase of Rom 11:25–26 (*caecitas ex parte contigit in Israel … et sic omnis Israel salvus fieret*), merged with Isa 10:22 / Rom 9:27 (*reliquiae salvae fient*).","certain")
n("n119","s004-12","ebreorum","la","textual","B: *hebreorum*.")
n("n120","s004-05","by divine inspiration","en","translation","A reads *diuina miseratione* (‘by divine mercy’); the translation follows B and the *Ordinatio*/Augustine, *inspiratione*.")
n("n121","s004-05","in his first letter to Jerome","en","translation","A’s *Dieronimum* corrected to Jerome (B *Hieronimum*). It is Augustine’s *Ep.* 82, not his first letter to Jerome; Gorris presumably means ‘first’ as quoted in the *Decretum*.")
n("n122","s004-05","I have learned","en","translation","Translating Augustine’s *didici* (B) for A’s corrupt *dedisti* (‘you have given’).")
n("n123","s004-06","This do, and thou shalt live","en","translation","Luke 10:28 (B *Lu. x*); A wrongly cites ‘Matthew 7’, and the reference has been omitted from the translation. Biblical quotations are given in the Douay-Rheims wording where possible.")
n("n124","s004-06","still await","en","translation","Following B *expectant*; A *spectant* (‘look to’) would also do.")
n("n125","s004-09","the brother of the King of Armenia","en","translation","Gorris’s description; historically Hayton was the king’s nephew. See Latin note on *Abynotus*.")
n("n126","s004-10","which ⟨will fall⟩, or falls, when …","en","translation","The Latin breaks off (*que dum ruet vel ruit*); the ellipsis marks the incomplete clause rather than an omission by the translator.")
n("n127","s004-10","while cadis is “mountain”","en","translation","Literally ‘but *deus cadis* is a mountain’; *deus* seems intrusive (see Latin note) and is not translated.")
n("n128","s004-11","by the year 1500","en","translation","So A; B has 1600.")
n("n129","s004-12","The remnant of Israel shall be converted in the end","en","translation","Gorris’s free version of Romans 11, translated literally.")

# --- s005 (Prologue q.3 = Ord. prol. p.3 qq.1–3)
n("n130","s005-01","Tertia prologi questio","la","textual","B prefixes the title *Vtrum theologia sit de deo vt de subiecto*, which A lacks. *Ord.* prol. p. 3 qq. 1–3, nn. 124 ff.")
n("n131","s005-01","forma inquit simplex subiectum esse non potest","la","source","Boethius, *De Trinitate* c. 2: *forma vero quae est sine materia non poterit esse subiectum*; cited at *Ord.* prol. n. 129.","certain")
n("n132","s005-01","sexto de trinitate","la","textual","Corrupt in both witnesses: A *sexto de trinitate*, B *aug. viii. de tri.*. The quotation is Augustine, *De civ. Dei* VIII.1 (*theologian … quo verbo Graeco significari intellegimus de divinitate rationem sive sermonem*), as at *Ord.* prol. n. 132 and as Gorris himself cites it correctly at §s005-05.","verified")
n("n133","s005-01","aspicit","la","textual","B: *respicit*.")
n("n134","s005-02","Egidius de Roma quodlibeto 3° q. 2","la","source","Giles of Rome, *Quodlibet* III q. 2 (ed. Louvain 1646): God as subject *sub ratione restauratoris / reparatoris*; cf. *Ord.* prol. n. 164 and n. 168. Exact question to be checked.","to-verify")
n("n135","s005-02","Et huic nihil potest poni","la","textual","A is corrupt; B reads *Vel posset poni … vt fr. Guilhel. var.*, which matches Scotus’s sequence of alternatives (*vel posset poni …*). The translation follows B.")
n("n136","s005-02","Henricus in summa","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Summa quaest. ord.* a. 19 (God as subject of theology *sub ratione deitatis*? / *sub ratione communi*); *Ord.* prol. n. 159 ff.","to-verify")
n("n137","s005-03","Bonauentura","la","source","The view that Christ (*Christus integer*, head and members) is the subject of theology is commonly attributed to Robert Kilwardby and Robert Grosseteste; Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* prooem. q. 1, names the *credibile* as subject but allows Christ as *subiectum integrale*. Gorris’s attribution to Bonaventure is therefore partly right. *Ord.* prol. n. 172.","to-verify")
n("n138","s005-04","Henricus de Gandano quodlibeto. 3. q. 6","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodlibet* III q. 6? Gorris’s identification of the source of *Ad tertiam quaestionem sic posset probabiliter dici* (*Ord.* prol. n. 196) is not yet verified.","to-verify")
n("n139","s005-05","Aug. 8° de ciui.","la","source","Augustine, *De civ. Dei* VIII.1. Correct here, cf. note on *sexto de trinitate*.","verified")
n("n140","s005-06","theologia in se","la","source","*Ord.* prol. nn. 141 ff. (theology in itself / in us; necessary and contingent theology; the primary subject virtually containing all truths of the habit). Gorris here quotes Scotus nearly verbatim, hence the quotation marks in the translation.","verified")
n("n141","s005-07","6° metha","la","source","Aristotle, *Metaph.* VI.1, 1026a21: *honorabilissimam scientiam oportet esse circa honorabilissimum genus*; *Ord.* prol. n. 157.","certain")
n("n142","s005-08","quadrupliciter","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 158: fourfold knowledge of man (proper; common; *per accidens*; relative). Gorris sorts Henry, Ware and Giles under these heads, which is his own (or his school’s) schematisation of nn. 159–168.","verified")
n("n143","s005-09","ens infinitum","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 168: the first object of our theology is *ens infinitum*, the most perfect concept of God available to us *pro statu isto*.","verified")
n("n144","s005-10","incarnatus in virgine","la","textual","B: *incarnatus a virgine*.")
n("n145","s005-11","ex propria motione earum","la","textual","A *ex propria motione earum*; B *ex proprie motione earum*, confirming *motione*. The blessed may have natural knowledge of creatures moved by the creatures themselves, besides seeing them in the divine essence (*Ord.* prol. n. 200 ff.).","verified")
n("n146","s005-11","beatus","la","textual","A prints *būs* (abbreviation); B has *beatus*, which confirms the expansion.","verified")
n("n147","s005-11","grammatica nec logica","la","textual","B: *grammatica siue logica*.")
n("n148","s005-01","[Augustine, The City of God] VIII, c. 1","en","translation","The translation silently corrects A’s ‘*De Trinitate* VI’ to Augustine, *De civitate Dei* VIII.1, the real source of the definition.")
n("n149","s005-02","Or [a science] could be posited under some attributal aspect","en","translation","Translated from B (*vel posset poni*), since A’s *Et huic nihil potest poni* gives no sense here.")
n("n150","s005-02","Restorer","en","translation","*reparator*: God as restorer of fallen nature, i.e. as Redeemer.")
n("n151","s005-08","Giles’s [by what is] relative","en","translation","Gorris’s compressed classification (*communis … accidentalis … respectiua*) is expanded in brackets so as to align it with the fourfold division just given.")
n("n152","s005-11","from their own motion [upon his intellect]","en","translation","*ex propria motione earum*: the creatures themselves moving the intellect as objects.")

# --- s006 (Prologue q.4 = Ord. prol. p.4 qq.1–2 and p.3 q.3? ; Gorris’s ‘fourth question’)
n("n153","s006-01","Quarta prologi questio","la","textual","B repeats the first words (dittography) at the start of the question.")
n("n154","s006-02","Fortasse non erunt cogitationes nostre volubiles","la","source","Augustine, *De Trinitate* XV.16.26: *Fortassis etiam non erunt volubiles nostrae cogitationes ab aliis in alia euntes atque redeuntes*; cited in Scotus’s Prologue, part 4. Checked against the Latin Library text.","verified")
n("n155","s006-03","Hanc questionem doctor superficialiter transit","la","textual","B: *doctor subtilis superficialiter*.")
n("n156","s006-03","lumine medio","la","source","The ‘intermediate light’ between faith and glory is Henry of Ghent’s theory of the *lumen medium* (*Summa* a. 13 q. 9?), which Scotus rejects. Gorris says the rejection is ‘in the *reportationes*’ (*reportatoriis*, confirmed by B), i.e. Scotus’s *Reportatio* (*Report. Paris.* prol.), not the *Ordinatio*: an interesting sign that he had a *Reportatio* to hand.","to-verify")
n("n157","s006-04","Varronis. 3. q. 1. li.","la","textual","So A (and substantially B): an inverted reference, presumably *li. 1 q. 3* (William of Ware, *In I Sent.* prol. q. 3), as the translation assumes.","conjecture")
n("n158","s006-04","improbatur","la","textual","B: *improbata*.")
n("n159","s006-05","prout sare diffinitur","la","textual","A *sare* is a misprint; B *prout scire definitur*, adopted in the translation. Aristotle, *Posterior Analytics* I.2, 71b9–12 (definition of *scire*).","verified")
n("n160","s006-05","quatuor conditiones","la","source","*Ord.* prol. n. 208: the four conditions of science in the strict sense (certain; of necessary truth; caused by evident prior necessary truth; applied by syllogistic discourse).","verified")
n("n161","s006-06","Circa scientiam oportet certificari","la","source","Aristotle, *EN* VI.3, 1139b18–24 (science of the necessary), paraphrased in the medieval *auctoritates* tradition.","certain")
n("n162","s006-06","6° metha. particularium","la","textual","B: *vi. metaph. et i. poste.*; the principle *singularium non est scientia* (cf. *Metaph.* VI.2, 1026b; VII.15).","uncertain")
n("n163","s006-06","Principia cognoscimus inquantum terminos cognoscimus","la","source","Aristotle, *APo* I.3, 72b23–25, in its medieval *auctoritas* form.","certain")
n("n164","s006-08","opinio theologie prima parte","la","textual","A *theologie* is a misreading of *Thome* (*Thō*); B *opinio thome*. Thomas, *ST* I q. 1 a. 2: sacred doctrine is subalternated to the science of God and the blessed. The translation reads ‘Thomas’.","verified")
n("n165","s006-08","sic. igitur","la","textual","A is elliptic; B has the full text *sunt de eisdem conclusionibus. sic igitur theologia nostra non est subalternata …*, which the translation supplies in brackets.")
n("n166","s006-08","numerus et sonus in musica","la","source","Aristotle, *APo* I.13, 78b32 ff. (harmonics subalternated to arithmetic); *Ord.* prol. p. 4.","certain")
n("n167","s006-02","discursus","en","translation","Latin *discursus* is kept in technical passages: the passage of the intellect from premisses to conclusion; Scotus distinguishes temporal discursus from discursus ‘in the order of nature’.")
n("n168","s006-03","reportationes","en","translation","Lat. *reportatoria*: the reports of Scotus’s lectures (*Reportatio*), as opposed to his revised *Ordinatio*.")
n("n169","s006-04","[Book] I, q. 3","en","translation","Translating the inverted reference *3. q. 1. li.* as Book I, q. 3; see Latin note.")
n("n170","s006-05","“to know”","en","translation","Following B *scire* for A’s *sare*.")
n("n171","s006-08","the opinion of Thomas","en","translation","A reads *theologie* (‘of theology’); the translation follows B *Thome*.")
n("n172","s006-08","are [about the same conclusions]","en","translation","Supplied from B, where A is elliptic (*sic.*).")
n("n173","s006-10","he speaks doubtfully","en","translation","Augustine’s *fortassis* in the quoted text (see §s006-02).")

